WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:13.250
SUN MICROSYSTEMS:
Sun Microsystems: the company that changed

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:17.210
the world and then disappeared into thin air.

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:22.539
We all know about revolutionary tech inventions
like the iPhone, the computer, the internet,

00:00:22.539 --> 00:00:27.830
and the cloud, but these were the guys who
made each and every one of these inventions

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:29.619
what they are today.

00:00:29.619 --> 00:00:33.510
For example, they’re the ones who created
the Java programming language.

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:39.520
While Java has declined in popularity in recent
times, it’s still one of the most influential

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.530
coding languages in the world.

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:46.399
It’s used in everything from laptops and
computers to gaming consoles, smartphones,

00:00:46.399 --> 00:00:47.989
and data centers.

00:00:47.989 --> 00:00:53.620
Not to mention, thousands of languages and
platforms have been heavily inspired by Java.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:56.039
Likely the best example is Android.

00:00:56.039 --> 00:01:00.440
In fact, Google ended up fighting a several
year long court battle after copying Java

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:02.379
code into Android.

00:01:02.379 --> 00:01:07.200
But, Java was just one of Sun’s many innovations.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:11.430
Sun is also the ones behind SPARC or scalable
processor architecture.

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:18.000
Again, this is probably something that most
of you have never heard of, but SPARC revolutionized

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.010
chip design.

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:23.270
With SPARC, Sun basically figured out how
to triple the number of instructions sets

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:25.340
that a given chip could handle.

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:29.630
And it wasn’t long until this technology
made it’s way into opensource projects,

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:34.350
super computers, and basically became the
new gold standard for chip design.

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:38.900
Speaking of gold standards, Sun also set the
gold standard when it comes to enterprise

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:40.579
software.

00:01:40.579 --> 00:01:45.350
Solaris is a Unix based operating system that
was created way back in 1993.

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:52.010
But, to this day, Solaris is the go to OS
for powering backend infrastructure.

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:55.280
They’re basically the windows of the backend.

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:59.590
But, all of that is just a handful of Sun’s
contributions.

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:04.659
They also played critical roles in the development
of virtual computers, virtualization, cloud

00:02:04.659 --> 00:02:08.560
computing, database management, super computers,
and so much more.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:15.239
But, despite their monumental role in tech,
not many people are familiar with Sun today.

00:02:15.239 --> 00:02:21.569
This is primarily because Oracle bought Sun
in 2009 and they basically killed the brand.

00:02:21.569 --> 00:02:26.510
But, while the Sun brand may no longer be
a thing, there’s no question that their

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:30.860
inventions and innovations will live on decades
if not centuries.

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:36.921
So, here’s the story of the company that
changed the world and then disappeared: Sun

00:02:36.921 --> 00:02:38.920
Microsystems.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:45.420
FOUNDING SUN:
Taking a look back, the story of Sun Microsystems

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:52.099
can be traced back in the early 1980s to a
Stanford PhD student named Andy Bechtolsheim.

00:02:52.099 --> 00:02:54.990
Andy was nothing less than a tech genius.

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:59.080
At Stanford his main project was creating
a powerful network that could link the various

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:01.160
departments of the university.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:06.250
He called this network the SUN workstation
with SUN standing for Stanford University

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:07.250
Network.

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:12.110
This network was extremely complex for the
time and required several managers, engineers,

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:17.239
and support staff, but it also linked together
computers like never before.

00:03:17.239 --> 00:03:22.600
Fun fact, two of these managers would steal
this tech and create Cisco which would go

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:27.450
onto be the world’s largest company but
that’s a wholenother story.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:32.319
Going back to Andy, while he was a genius,
we can’t give him all the credit as he did

00:03:32.319 --> 00:03:35.820
have a bunch of help from our good old friends
Xerox.

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:38.200
Xerox was honestly just idiots.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:43.540
They had no conception what intellectual property
was or why they should protect IP.

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:48.420
One of the most notable examples of this was
their no fee consultant program.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:52.930
Essentially, instead of paying consultants
with cash, Xerox opted to give consultants

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:54.020
free access to their research.

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:55.140
You can bet that Andy leveraged this program
to the absolute max when creating the SUN

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:56.140
workstation.

00:03:56.140 --> 00:04:00.012
Almost instantly, the SUN workstation attracted
a bunch of scientists and engineers who were

00:04:00.012 --> 00:04:03.189
willing to pay big bucks to use the workstation.

00:04:03.189 --> 00:04:08.739
So, Andy would nonchalantly price licenses
to his workstation at $10,000 each.

00:04:08.739 --> 00:04:13.370
That’s equivalent to $33,000 today.

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:18.400
Little to say, Andy was racking in the dough
in 1981 and it wasn’t long until he drew

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:23.669
the attention of a few MBA graduates: Vinod
Khosla and Scott McNealy.

00:04:23.669 --> 00:04:29.410
Basically, they told Andy, “What you have
here is great, but join us and we can make

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:30.669
this revolutionary.”

00:04:30.669 --> 00:04:33.199
And that’s exactly what they did.

00:04:33.199 --> 00:04:40.070
On February 24, 1982, the trio would establish
SUN microsystems based on the SUN workstation.

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:44.500
Bill Joy was one of the first employees to
join the company and he has since been credited

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:46.440
as a cofounder as well.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:48.910
And with that, Sun was in business.

00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:54.880
They began selling their Sun-1 and Sun-2 workstations
which were largely inspired by Andy’s original

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:59.620
workstation, and let’s just say, these sold
like hot cakes.

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:04.570
Basically every university in the world wanted
one of these workstations leading to $8 million

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:06.700
in sales in the first year alone.

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:12.310
Also, we should note that unlike many startups
today that burn money for decades, Sun achieved

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:15.060
profitability within its first quarter.

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:18.780
This gave them quite a bit of leverage when
it came recruiting the best talent.

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:22.280
One of their first hires were Eric Schmidt
and Carol Bartz.

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:27.100
If you’re not familiar with these two, Eric
would go on to be the CEO of Google and Carol

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:29.850
would go on to be the CEO of Yahoo.

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:36.510
So, Sun had a pretty strong team to say the
least, but they were just getting started.

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:41.139
Virtually all of their revenue was coming
from universities and research institutions,

00:05:41.139 --> 00:05:46.370
and while that was already super lucrative,
they knew that the real money was with companies

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:48.930
and that was their real target.

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:54.720
THE BRIGHT SUN:
Hearing about all these super successful people,

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:59.590
I know a lot ya’ll wonder why they never
stop to just enjoy life.

00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:04.979
For example, why did Elon gamble his PayPal
money on Tesla and SpaceX and why is he still

00:06:04.979 --> 00:06:07.380
working 120 hours every week.

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:12.810
Well, I don’t know the answer to this, but
what I do know is that one of Sun's founders,

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:16.280
Vinod Khosla, would do exactly what ya’ll
have been dreaming about.

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:21.550
Just 2 years after founding Sun, at age 29,
Vinod would become a multi millionaire and

00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:22.870
he just straight up retired.

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:28.930
Since then, he would just spend his time investing
in startups which has made him a multi billionaire.

00:06:28.930 --> 00:06:33.960
So, looks like Vinod was able to get of the
hedonic treadmill early on, but the rest of

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:36.110
Sun was just getting started.

00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:42.250
Sun would turn their focus onto design companies
that relied on CAD models, specifically Computervision.

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:46.710
Computervision already had a contract with
Sun’s biggest competitor: Apollo Computer.

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:50.290
But, Sun wasn’t willing to accept this as
a lost opportunity.

00:06:50.290 --> 00:06:55.650
They would aggressively pitch Sun and make
offers over and over and over again until

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:58.170
Computervision finally agreed to give them
a shot.

00:06:58.170 --> 00:07:04.060
For Computervision, Sun was just one of their
dozens of backend partners, but for Sun this

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:09.160
partnership was gamechanging as it established
them as a reputable player.

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:13.240
Other companies started thinking: If Sun is
good enough for Computervision, it must be

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:14.990
good enough for us too.

00:07:14.990 --> 00:07:19.379
And this was exactly the push Sun needed to
break into the enterprise market.

00:07:19.379 --> 00:07:22.530
There was only one more major market left:
governments.

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:28.169
In 1984, Sun would open a new subsidiary called
Sun Federal and it accomplished exactly what

00:07:28.169 --> 00:07:29.169
you would think.

00:07:29.169 --> 00:07:31.521
It sold workstations to the government.

00:07:31.521 --> 00:07:35.650
Given that Sun already had quite a bit of
notoriety amongst schools and companies, breaking

00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:38.320
into the government market wasn’t too challenging.

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:44.080
In fact, by 1991, Sun was delivering over
half of all workstations ordered by local,

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:46.400
state, and federal governments.

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:51.259
All of this allowed Sun to become the fasted
growing American company between 1985 and

00:07:51.259 --> 00:07:56.060
1989 averaging growth of 145% per year.

00:07:56.060 --> 00:08:01.280
This meant that Sun was able to achieve $1
billion worth of revenue within just 6 years

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:02.860
of being founded.

00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:07.990
Clearly, Sun was a serious a company that
had a stronghold in a multitude of markets.

00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:13.220
But, it’s what Sun would do next with all
of this influence and power that really made

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:14.449
them legendary.

00:08:14.449 --> 00:08:21.460
THE SUN NEVER SETS:
As companies mature, they usually get greedier

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:23.300
and greedier.

00:08:23.300 --> 00:08:28.310
They become enthralled in trying to maximize
market share, maximize profits, and maximize

00:08:28.310 --> 00:08:29.690
the stock price.

00:08:29.690 --> 00:08:35.380
Of course, Sun also wanted to accomplish this,
but giving back to the tech world was arguably

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:37.600
of equal importance for them.

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:43.779
One of their first revolutions was the Network
File System or NFS in 1984.

00:08:43.779 --> 00:08:48.560
Before NFS, file sharing systems were usually
limited to specific processors and specific

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:50.810
operating systems.

00:08:50.810 --> 00:08:55.000
Companies did this to maximize the sales of
their own hardware similar to what Apple does

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:56.000
today.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:00.240
But, Sun threw this ideology completely out
the window with NFS.

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:06.690
NFS allowed for data sharing regardless of
processor type, OS, or communication system.

00:09:06.690 --> 00:09:13.070
Unsurprisingly, this became the industry standard
within no time leading us to SPARC.

00:09:13.070 --> 00:09:17.990
Until the introduction of SPARC, virtually
all chips were built on CISC or Complex Instruction

00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:19.440
Set Computing architecture.

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:25.350
SPARC, however, was based on RISC or Reduced
Instruction Set Computing architecture.

00:09:25.350 --> 00:09:30.790
RISC was not only more powerful and cheaper,
but more importantly, it was magnitudes more

00:09:30.790 --> 00:09:36.380
scalable, so switching to SPARC based workstations
was a no brainer for customers.

00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:39.360
But, Sun didn’t just stop right there.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:44.730
Naturally, when you have control of such technology,
you’d want to patent the hell out of it

00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:49.899
and make sure that no one else gets access
to it, but Sun would do the exact opposite.

00:09:49.899 --> 00:09:55.790
In fact, Sun would begin licensing their RISC
design to other chip makers for cheap prices

00:09:55.790 --> 00:09:59.870
before they even launched their own RISC based
workstation.

00:09:59.870 --> 00:10:03.800
Their reasoning was that if they really wanted
to push forward the tech industry and make

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:07.329
RISC the new standard, they needed all the
help they could get.

00:10:07.329 --> 00:10:10.380
It doesn’t stop right there either.

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:15.140
Sun would actually go on to handover the rights
to SPARC to a non profit organization that

00:10:15.140 --> 00:10:17.890
was jointly owned by a bunch of computer companies.

00:10:17.890 --> 00:10:22.700
To be honest, I can’t think of another tech
giant that has voluntarily given away such

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:24.620
technology since then.

00:10:24.620 --> 00:10:27.330
Let me know if you can think of one in comments
below.

00:10:27.330 --> 00:10:32.769
Anyway, all of this brings us into Sun’s
most notable contribution: Java.

00:10:32.769 --> 00:10:37.660
Java was the brainchild of Sun engineer James
Gosling with substantial support from Sun

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:39.959
CEO Scott McNealy.

00:10:39.959 --> 00:10:45.519
Scott believed that personal computers were
just a blip in history and that the real invention

00:10:45.519 --> 00:10:46.519
network computing.

00:10:46.519 --> 00:10:51.560
Back in the 1990s, he predicted that everything
from refrigerators and telephones to credit

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:55.639
cards and door locks would all be connected
by a network.

00:10:55.639 --> 00:10:59.810
He wanted all the backend infrastructure that
was powering these technologies to be run

00:10:59.810 --> 00:11:05.060
on Java, and to accomplish that, in classic
Sun style he would opensource Java.

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:10.889
Let’s just say, Scott was spot on with his
vision and he more than accomplished his goal

00:11:10.889 --> 00:11:12.660
with Java.

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:17.890
It looks like giving away all this tech didn’t
take away from Sun throughout 90s as Sun would

00:11:17.890 --> 00:11:21.600
achieve a peak market cap of $200 billion.

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:26.820
But, unfortunately, the Sun would eventually
set.

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:32.899
A SUPERNOVA:
The bottomline is that all of Sun’s generous

00:11:32.899 --> 00:11:35.910
sharing eventually came back to bite them.

00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:40.320
You see, even though Sun opensourced a bunch
of their greatest tech throughout 90s, it

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:44.670
didn’t really hurt them then because companies
would opt to buy the official Sun version

00:11:44.670 --> 00:11:46.050
of the product.

00:11:46.050 --> 00:11:50.730
This was thanks to loads of VC funding and
entrepreneurs with sky high visions.

00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:55.620
But, after the dotcom bubble popped, this
reality quickly crumbled away.

00:11:55.620 --> 00:12:00.540
All of a sudden, the cheaper workstations
from Dell and IBM based on the same tech seemed

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:01.770
a lot more attractive.

00:12:01.770 --> 00:12:04.540
So, customers ditched Sun in droves.

00:12:04.540 --> 00:12:08.100
And as Sun tried to compete against these
lower cost alternatives, their profit margins

00:12:08.100 --> 00:12:10.800
would get slashed driving them into unprofitability.

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:16.410
Pretty soon, Sun fell from being the market
leader with a $200 billion valuation to being

00:12:16.410 --> 00:12:19.830
the drowning giant that needed a bailout.

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:24.930
IBM would offer $7 billion for Sun, but it
was Oracle that ended up snatching the company

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:27.110
for $7.4 billion.

00:12:27.110 --> 00:12:32.430
Once Oracle took over, the soul of Sun was
basically destroyed completely.

00:12:32.430 --> 00:12:36.350
Instead of keeping up Sun’s tradition of
contributing to and advancing the tech world,

00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:41.529
Oracle would focus on milking Sun for maximum
profit, but can you really blame them?

00:12:41.529 --> 00:12:46.329
Sun had already tried to do the right thing
and make technology as accessible as possible.

00:12:46.329 --> 00:12:52.130
But, while this strategy worked out fine during
good times, as soon as bad times hit, they

00:12:52.130 --> 00:12:56.570
were the first to get slaughtered and that’s
the story of the company that changed the

00:12:56.570 --> 00:12:57.899
world and disappeared.

00:12:57.899 --> 00:13:01.240
Were you guys familiar with Sun Microsystems?

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:02.290
Comment that down below.

00:13:02.290 --> 00:13:06.870
Also, drop a like if you respect Sun for trying
to do something that no other tech company

00:13:06.870 --> 00:13:08.839
has dared to attempt.

